From: Xiaofeng Yan <yanxiaofeng@example.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Feb 2026 14:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: mpt3sas: Disable high_iops_queues when online CPUs
 exceed available MSI-X vectors

When high_iops_queues are enabled on AERO/SEA controllers (e.g. SAS3816,
SAS3916 used in Broadcom 9500 series HBAs), the driver sets
irq_affinity.pre_vectors = high_iops_queues (8). These pre_vectors are
excluded from the kernel's managed CPU affinity spreading in
pci_alloc_irq_vectors_affinity(). This means only
(msix_vector_count - high_iops_queues) vectors participate in the
automatic CPU-to-IRQ affinity assignment.

On systems where num_online_cpus() >= msix_vector_count (e.g., a dual-
socket server with 128 CPUs using a 9500-8i HBA that supports max 128
MSI-X vectors), only 128 - 8 = 120 vectors get CPU affinity spreading
for 128 CPUs. The remaining 8 CPUs must share reply queues with other
CPUs, leading to:

  - Unbalanced I/O completion distribution across CPUs
  - Uneven interrupt load and throughput
  - Unexpected I/O merging behavior changes

This was observed on a Hygon S435 dual-socket system (128 CPUs) with
9500-8i HBA: FIO bandwidth tests showed significant imbalance across 36
HDDs. Setting max_msix_vectors=128 (which implicitly disables
high_iops_queues) resolved the issue. The same HBA on an Intel 4313
single-socket system (32 CPUs) showed balanced results because
32 + 8 = 40 << 128, leaving plenty of room.

Fix this by checking whether enabling high_iops_queues would leave
insufficient MSI-X vectors for 1:1 CPU mapping. When
num_online_cpus() + MPT3SAS_HIGH_IOPS_REPLY_QUEUES > msix_vector_count,
disable high_iops_queues to ensure every CPU gets a dedicated reply
queue vector.

Signed-off-by: Xiaofeng Yan <yanxiaofeng@example.com>
---
 drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c b/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c
index xxxxxxxxxxxx..yyyyyyyyyyyy 100644
--- a/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c
+++ b/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c
@@ -3340,9 +3340,30 @@ _base_check_and_enable_high_iops_queues(struct MPT3SAS_ADAPTER *ioc,
 	if (!reset_devices && ioc->is_aero_ioc &&
 	    hba_msix_vector_count == MPT3SAS_GEN35_MAX_MSIX_QUEUES &&
 	    num_online_cpus() >= MPT3SAS_HIGH_IOPS_REPLY_QUEUES &&
-	    max_msix_vectors == -1)
+	    max_msix_vectors == -1) {
+		/*
+		 * When high_iops_queues are enabled, these queues are set as
+		 * .pre_vectors in irq_affinity descriptor and won't be included
+		 * in the kernel managed CPU affinity spreading. This leaves
+		 * only (hba_msix_vector_count - high_iops_queues) vectors for
+		 * CPU affinity assignment. If online CPUs exceed this number,
+		 * some CPUs will have to share reply queues, resulting in
+		 * unbalanced I/O completions across CPUs.
+		 *
+		 * Disable high_iops_queues when there are not enough MSI-X
+		 * vectors left to provide 1:1 mapping for all online CPUs.
+		 */
+		if (num_online_cpus() + MPT3SAS_HIGH_IOPS_REPLY_QUEUES >
+		    hba_msix_vector_count) {
+			ioc->high_iops_queues = 0;
+			ioc_info(ioc,
+			    "Disabled high_iops_queues: online CPUs (%d) + "
+			    "high_iops_queues (%d) exceeds max MSI-X vectors (%d)\n",
+			    num_online_cpus(),
+			    MPT3SAS_HIGH_IOPS_REPLY_QUEUES,
+			    hba_msix_vector_count);
+			return;
+		}
 		ioc->high_iops_queues = MPT3SAS_HIGH_IOPS_REPLY_QUEUES;
-	else
+	} else
 		ioc->high_iops_queues = 0;
 }
 
